---
// Swarm testing (#418 (technique pages, part 2)): eight seeds, each with the
// fault families it drew. The recorded runs are src/runs/swarm.json;
// public/swarm.js draws the table from each run's families.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Technique from "../../../layouts/Technique.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { constNames, excerpt, repoFile } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../../../runs/swarm.json";

const engine = repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/engine.rs");
const families = { network: constNames(engine, "NETWORK_FAULTS"), storage: constNames(engine, "STORAGE_FAULTS") };
const mode = excerpt(engine, "let mode = if swarm {", "out.push(Chaos::Storage(mode));", 1);
---
<Technique
  title="Swarm testing"
  description="Each seed draws its own set of faults. A lot of a few finds more than a little of each."
  guide={[
    "part3-building/28-swarm-testing.md",
    "part3-building/07-chaos.md",
    "appendix/04-fault-reference.md",
  ]}
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>With every fault family on in every run, each family gets a small share of the run. Some bugs need one
    fault many times, with no other fault in the way: a long partition and no reboot, or a disk that fails
    again and again. When each run has a little of everything, these runs never come.</p>

  <h2>The technique</h2>
  <p>With <code>ChaosMode::Swarm</code>, each seed switches each fault family off with a probability of one
    half. The families that stay on get all of the run. Over many seeds, each family is on in about half of
    the runs, and alone or with few others in some of them. The seed decides the set, so the set replays
    with the seed.</p>
  <p>The simulator records the families of each seed in a <code>chaos_families</code> event. It is read-only:
    it draws nothing and does not change the run.</p>

  <h2>The live run</h2>
  <p>Eight seeds of the same register, with network faults, storage faults and reboots in swarm mode. Each
    row is one seed: a dot for each family that is on. Columns 1 to {families.network.length} are the
    network families, columns a to {String.fromCharCode(96 + families.storage.length)} the storage families.
    Hover a column for its name. Select a row to open its run in the player.</p>
  <div class="breakout">
    <section class="run" id="run" aria-label="A live run"></section>
    <div class="run run-under">
      <div class="run-head label"><span>the fault families of each seed</span></div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table class="run-table swarm-table" id="swarm-table"></table></div>
      <p class="run-out" id="swarm-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <ol class="fam-legend label">
        {families.network.map((f, i) => <li><b>{i + 1}</b> {f}</li>)}
        {families.storage.map((f, i) => <li><b>{String.fromCharCode(97 + i)}</b> {f}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
    <noscript><p>The live run needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify(runs).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="swarm-families" set:html={JSON.stringify(families)} />

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>One mode for each surface. The site's engine picks <code>Swarm</code> or <code>Random</code> for the
    network and the storage:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={mode} />
  <p>A test can also remove families for each seed, with <code>network_fault_mask</code> and
    <code>storage_fault_mask</code>: a mask applies after the swarm draw and draws nothing.</p>
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url("swarm.js")}></script>
</Technique>
